## 1. SIBLING PARITY -- the edited arm against its siblings and against the byte-endianness idiom

The table printer in main() has four NULL arms, each with a matching early return in loadstore():
  arm A  store && signedness          main :239-242   loadstore :98-99    128 NULLs   no legal encoding (no signed store)
  arm B  !registeroffset && usr       main :244-247   loadstore :107-108   96 NULLs   .usr exists only with [rS2]/rS2
  arm C  !registeroffset && scaledness main :249-252  loadstore :110-111   48 NULLs   scaling exists only with rS2
  arm D  size == 0 && scaledness      main :254-257   loadstore :104-105   24 NULLs   THE EDITED ARM -- a LEGAL encoding
         (counts: packet.md section 7's census, reproduced by BASELINE.out section 4: 296 = 128 + 96 + 48 + 24)
The asymmetry: arms A-C NULL cells no legal instruction encodes; arm D NULLs cells the manual defines
(mc88100.txt:6423-6429, xmem.bu rD,rS1[rS2]; :2960-2965 "For byte accesses ... the result is identical") and that
four sites index unguarded or into ic->f (cpu_m88k_instr.c:2377/:2395 and :2411/:2426, xmem_slow; :3622, the register-
form ld/st decoder; CHECKS #3).

The NEAREST sibling is not A-C but the BYTE-ENDIANNESS idiom: loadstore() :101-102 returns early for size == 0 &&
endianness (no big-endian byte definitions), print_function_name() :74-75 prints "_le" for a big-endian byte, and
main() has NO NULL arm for it -- so the big-endian byte slots alias the little-endian byte handlers.  Arm D had the same
two halves (early return :104-105; :80 omits "_scaled" for size 0) PLUS a NULL arm.  The fix removes only the NULL arm,
so arm D becomes the endianness idiom exactly.  Rows that pin the parity: the 12 BE pins (176..504) expect "_le" names
(the idiom in effect inside the edited cells); the cparts at 144/148/152, 208/212/216, 400/404/408, 464/468/472 pin the
unedited big-endian byte aliases.
Owed sentences (no row): arms A-C are held ONLY by the NULL-count row -- a NULL moved within them keeps 272 -- and no
per-slot pin is rowed for them (clause (e), disclosed in the driver's contract header).  loadstore()'s :104-105 is KEPT
(A2); the rows that see its deletion are G3 and A5 (S1 below), never a name pin.

